+### Stereoscopic Rendering
+Rendering from multiple viewpoints (offset by a fixed rotation) to enhance user perception of depth features, used for live stereoscopic display [S4]. Stereoscopic rendering required an additional 2.5ms computational cost on the NVIDIA GTX Titan Black GPU [S4].
 
 ## Background
-The specific applications and integrators described here span a wide range of domains. Customization Volume Integration and Cutting Plane Integration address interactive visualization needs in medical and scientific imaging, allowing users to dynamically adjust appearance and visibility [S1]. TVAM Integrators are specific to additive manufacturing simulation, where the physics of light projection and curing must be modeled [S1]. Cinematic Rendering and Colored Stochastic Shadow Mapping bring photo-realism and global illumination to medical imaging, moving beyond standard DVR [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. Directional Occlusion Shading enhances perceptual cues in interactive rendering [S2, unofficial]. Compositional Rendering and Virtual Magic Lantern address user interaction and scene composition in emerging neural rendering and VR contexts [S1]. The Two Level Volume Rendering with Layers technique offers an alternative compositing strategy for multi-material datasets [S1]. The Sobierajski/Kaufman framework represents an early comprehensive approach to volumetric ray tracing with global illumination [S2, unofficial]. Pseudo-depth Feature Rendering is a recent development in the context of neural radiance fields and feature distillation [S3].
+The concept of specific applications in volume rendering emerged as the technique moved from academic research to industrial and medical practice. Early work by Sobierajski and Kaufman established a comprehensive ray tracing framework for global illumination in volumes, integrated into the VolVis system [S2, unofficial]. In medical imaging, the demand for photo-realism led to Cinematic Rendering, which adapts path tracing from computer graphics to solve the complex light-matter interactions in biological tissues [9473-cinematic-rendering.md]. The need for better interaction metaphors led to techniques like the Virtual Magic Lantern [11335-virtual-magic-lantern.md] and Two-Level Volume Rendering with Layers [S1], which allow users to inspect specific regions with different visual styles. More recently, with the rise of additive manufacturing and neural rendering, integrators have been specialized for TVAM optimization [6814-tvam-integrator.md] and feature field distillation [S3].
 
